  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nDeparture of high pressure makes room for a cutoff low pressure\nsystem to advance northward from the Carolinas beginning today.\nIncreased cloudiness has already been noted on satellite at the\ntime of writing, with further vertical growth starting to \ntranspire. Onshore flow developing in the wake of the surface \nhigh will continuously feed moisture into the region this week, \nsetting off an active weather pattern. \n\nFocusing on today, the northern fringes of the advancing cutoff\nlow will bring shower and storm chances back into the Kanawha\nValley through the afternoon. The loss of daytime heating at\nsunset will chop off further development and longevity of\nstorms, but until then lightning and a quick downpour will be\nfeature with today's activity. Patchy river valley fog and\nincreased clouds will then round out the night into Wednesday\nmorning.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nAn active weather pattern settles over the Central Appalachians\nfor the rest of the week and into the weekend. The\naforementioned cutoff low featured in Key Message 1 will work in\ntandem with longwave troughing aloft to promote daily chances\nfor afternoon/evening showers and storms across the region.\nEmbedded shortwave energy drifting across the parent trough will\nlead to additional contribution for convective activity this\nweek, but as it currently stands there is no strong signal for\nwidespread severe weather this week. \n\nCumulus fields will bloom each afternoon as convective \ntemperatures are reached in the early afternoon, followed by\nfurther intensification to showers and storms during peak\nheating hours. Isolated damaging wind gusts and heavy downpours will\nbe the main features with activity this week, and could result\nin localized high water issues, dependent on placement of storms\nand antecedent soil conditions each day.",
